  data-image-dimensions="4928x3280"
  data-image-focal-point="0.5,0.5"
  data-sqsp-image-classic-block-image
  alt=""
  data-licensed-asset-preview="false"
  data-load="false" elementtiming="system-image-block" src="https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/615d726d2ca5967ad6bcaf49/1643924350240-4JPEQBAS9YFQFJAOGNWL/Items+found+in+a+house+in+Brackley+-+1860s-80s.jpg" width="4928" height="3280" alt="" sizes="auto, (max-width: 640px) 100vw, (max-width: 767px) 100.00vw, 100.00vw" style="display:block;object-position: var(--image-component-focal-point);object-fit: var(--image-component-object-fit);" srcset="https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/615d726d2ca5967ad6bcaf49/1643924350240-4JPEQBAS9YFQFJAOGNWL/Items+found+in+a+house+in+Brackley+-+1860s-80s.jpg?format=100w 100w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/615d726d2ca5967ad6bcaf49/1643924350240-4JPEQBAS9YFQFJAOGNWL/Items+found+in+a+house+in+Brackley+-+1860s-80s.jpg?format=300w 300w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/615d726d2ca5967ad6bcaf49/1643924350240-4JPEQBAS9YFQFJAOGNWL/Items+found+in+a+house+in+Brackley+-+1860s-80s.jpg?format=500w 500w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/615d726d2ca5967ad6bcaf49/1643924350240-4JPEQBAS9YFQFJAOGNWL/Items+found+in+a+house+in+Brackley+-+1860s-80s.jpg?format=750w 750w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/615d726d2ca5967ad6bcaf49/1643924350240-4JPEQBAS9YFQFJAOGNWL/Items+found+in+a+house+in+Brackley+-+1860s-80s.jpg?format=1000w 1000w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/615d726d2ca5967ad6bcaf49/1643924350240-4JPEQBAS9YFQFJAOGNWL/Items+found+in+a+house+in+Brackley+-+1860s-80s.jpg?format=1500w 1500w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/615d726d2ca5967ad6bcaf49/1643924350240-4JPEQBAS9YFQFJAOGNWL/Items+found+in+a+house+in+Brackley+-+1860s-80s.jpg?format=2500w 2500w" loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-loader="sqs" data-image-component="1.0.1">
        </div>
      </div>
    
        
      

      
        <figcaption
          data-sqsp-image-classic-block-caption-container
          
          class="image-caption-wrapper"
        >
          <div class="image-caption"><p class="" style="white-space:pre-wrap;"><em>Items including a pair of shoes, bottles, a thimble, and a leather purse found in a house in Brackley, Northamptonshire - 1860s-80s.</em></p></div>
        </figcaption>
      
    
      </figure>
    

    


  </div>

  

</div></div><div class="sqs-block website-component-block sqs-block-website-component sqs-block-html html-block" data-block-css="[&quot;https://definitions.sqspcdn.com/website-component-definition/static-assets/website.components.html/af6efbdf-9dbd-47ae-a709-72f32cc583ea_1002/website.components.html.styles.css&quot;]" data-block-scripts="[&quot;https://definitions.sqspcdn.com/website-component-definition/static-assets/website.components.html/af6efbdf-9dbd-47ae-a709-72f32cc583ea_1002/website.components.html.visitor.js&quot;]" data-block-type="1337" data-definition-name="website.components.html" data-sqsp-block="text" data-website-component-id="yui_3_17_2_1_1643923056310_29095" id="block-yui_3_17_2_1_1643923056310_29095"><div class="sqs-block-content"><div class="sqs-text-block-container">
  
  <div class="sqs-html-content" data-sqsp-text-block-content><p class="" style="white-space:pre-wrap;">In most cases the house is older than the shoes found. I have had many a conversation with the finder who believes that as their house is seventeenth century the shoes must be the same age. When I’ve told them are much later for example from the 1850s, they say this is impossible as no alterations or building activity has taken place in that area since the house was built, but it clearly has been accessed at some point to explain the presence of the shoes. It is thought that it is unlikely that the occupants of the house hid the shoes but a practice that builders might do this during renovations to bring good luck in their work. It also raises the interesting question of what has happened to the shoe.&nbsp;</p><p class="" style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The research undertaken by The University of Hertfordshire in particularly <a href="https://theconcealedrevealed.wordpress.com" target="_blank">Professor Owen Davies and Dr Ceri Houlbrook</a>, currently suggests less and less that they were specifically apotropaic items, ones that have the power to avert evil influences or bad luck – and that they were connected to notions of good luck and hope.&nbsp;</p><p class="" style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The popularity of the subject and the questions it raises that still cannot be fully answered make it such an intriguing subject open to different interpretation.</p></div>


  
